#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184004 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b47fa40d-613a-5e9b-ba07-c9057dd1a96e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals on Which a Function Is Concave Up or Down"}] :question/id #uuid "283001f5-f9e4-5990-a900-318677a48601" :question/math-academy-id "e-3740" :question/problem "Determine the intervals on which the function $f(x) = x^3 -3x$ is concave up." :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x)$ is concave up on an interval $(a,b)$ if $f''(x)> 0$ for all $x \\in (a,b).$\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x)= x^3 -3x \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}-3 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find where the function is concave up, we solve the inequality $f''(x) > 0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x & >0 \\\\ x & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is concave up on $(0, \\infty).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184005 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "437c168a-6799-5d13-bd9b-031290a84da6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals of Concavity of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "006fe48a-9c8e-568b-90f1-40b12dc338bb" :question/math-academy-id "e-3741" :question/problem "Find the intervals of concavity for the function $f(x) = 2 x^3 - x^4.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =2{x}^{3}-{x}^{4} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =6{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =12x-12{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve $f''(x) > 0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x-12{x}^{2} & >0 \\\\ 12{x}^{2}-12x & <0 \\\\ x(x-1) & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality by considering the graph of $y=x(x-1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/assets/54ae61b315630925ec612500a451a90f04b19cf0f5e455c2f3db597c79cebfa1.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $x(x-1) < 0$ when $0 < x < 1.$   So the graph is concave up on the interval  $(0,1).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when $f''(x) <          0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $x(x-1)  >        0,$ which has the solution $(-\\infty,0)$ and $\\left(1,\\infty\\right).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184006 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1b60735-9dae-5c23-991d-0241c208b1b7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals of Concavity Given the Graph of the Second Derivative"}] :question/id #uuid "99e07b9d-aaaa-54b6-9118-78df023261f3" :question/math-academy-id "e-3745" :question/problem "The graph below shows $y=g''(x),$ where the function $g(x)$ is defined on $[-5,5].$ Find the intervals on which the function $g(x)$ is concave up. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/assets/a4d19390678a977117b32211d87df80f15e863fbe93d4068bab0a9bf1080cb0b.png)" :question/worked-solution "A function $g(x)$ is concave up over an interval $(a,b)$ if $g''(x)> 0$ for all $x \\in (a,b).$\n\nOn the given graph of $y=g''(x),$ we can see that $g''(x) >0$ on the interval $(-1,5).$ Therefore, the function $g(x)$ is concave up on the interval $(-1,5).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303129 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1b60735-9dae-5c23-991d-0241c208b1b7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals of Concavity Given the Graph of the Second Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b34c298e-5082-53ae-bbcc-9abd9d3deebd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,0)$ and  $(0,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303131} {:answer/id #uuid "0fd39f9b-7cd6-5cbd-a639-497db45e5e5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303132} {:answer/id #uuid "d89c0caa-656e-59de-873e-5402af7b4273" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303133} {:answer/id #uuid "81f1adcc-62d5-51f4-80b1-cd547bd48eca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303134} {:answer/id #uuid "df90ad81-7c50-537c-9708-3715bba0d2e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-\\infty ,-1)$ and  $(1,\\infty )$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303135}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b34c298e-5082-53ae-bbcc-9abd9d3deebd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,0)$ and  $(0,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303131} :answer-field/id #uuid "197f6263-c641-5680-8632-5bff24d869ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303130}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cd8f4bb2-7373-5980-915b-81559c92c88f" :question/math-academy-id "q-114060" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/assets/c528a8bdbb9bc6dd1a9895109fdc6818b70787c5024c78e06e2d2ec3997777d6.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y={f}^{″}(x).$ Find the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave up." :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x)$ is concave up over an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nOn the given graph of $y={f}^{″}(x),$ we can see that ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ on the intervals  $(-1,0)$ and  $(0,1).$  Therefore, the function $f(x)$ is concave up on the intervals  $(-1,0)$ and  $(0,1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303136 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1b60735-9dae-5c23-991d-0241c208b1b7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals of Concavity Given the Graph of the Second Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eec5bffe-f37b-542a-b883-24981acf56b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,1)$ and $(2,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303138} {:answer/id #uuid "830cf24c-0dfa-5396-817d-7d783a1a481a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,1)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303139} {:answer/id #uuid "7495b2c2-665b-517b-a60b-9c61ed3ea1be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,1)$ and $(1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303140} {:answer/id #uuid "c14260ea-f10e-52ce-bcc9-bed40a408717" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,3)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303141} {:answer/id #uuid "426ba1bd-081f-552d-a1fe-5c1a630db5c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303142}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "426ba1bd-081f-552d-a1fe-5c1a630db5c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303142} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2a4d10c-c21d-558f-b943-31033714e15e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303137}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a0161bc1-4160-5449-a401-29481660be7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-114063" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/assets/9ac6bf6978169aaa3a29c570234cdbf26f37b6e91488d8e27c63255b8cbb3ba6.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y={f}^{″}(x),$ where the function $f(x)$ is defined on $[0,3].$ Find the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave down." :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x)$ is concave down over an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nOn the given graph of $y={f}^{″}(x),$ we can see that ${f}^{″}(x)<0$ on the interval  $(1,2)$ only. Therefore, the function $f(x)$ is concave down on the interval $(1,2)$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293687 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "437c168a-6799-5d13-bd9b-031290a84da6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals of Concavity of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ffde426-a97f-5dd2-afa0-be7cd1c04dca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293689} {:answer/id #uuid "57a153c2-4712-5abd-aeb8-86da041cec06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293690} {:answer/id #uuid "03d4f9be-e445-52f2-9e85-2b5fc15af3e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293691} {:answer/id #uuid "66e76bc3-16ac-5dbd-8410-79bf932a02c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293692} {:answer/id #uuid "2831d3e9-88ae-5a2f-8f3a-5c0526a007bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2831d3e9-88ae-5a2f-8f3a-5c0526a007bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293693} :answer-field/id #uuid "71107757-ea07-51d8-90b3-bf50cdd589cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293688}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7af01e6f-dc86-5725-9b0b-63602f01e9af" :question/math-academy-id "q-114270" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+4x+5}{{e}^{x}}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-1,1)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,-1)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,\\infty )$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-({x}^{2}+4x+5){e}^{-x}+(2x+4){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2x+1){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =-(x+1)^{2}{e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(x+1)^{2}{e}^{-x}-2(x+1){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-1){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =(x-1)(x+1){e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)(x+1){e}^{-x} & >0 \\\\ (x-1)(x+1) & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality by considering the graph of $y=(x+1)(x-1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/assets/09b4b237ec87a63e6de47eea5ed9156febbed0271dd0688c0be1c22a62c50c4e.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $(x+1)(x-1)>0$ on  $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and $(1,\\infty ).$  So the function is concave up on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and $(1,\\infty ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $(x+1)(x-1)<0,$ which has the solution $(-1,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320447 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "437c168a-6799-5d13-bd9b-031290a84da6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals of Concavity of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "560f6e49-18ff-5174-980c-720bcf8ef38f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320449} {:answer/id #uuid "22c091c6-f7b5-5527-b8b1-c873c7d06dd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320450} {:answer/id #uuid "957940e7-7f35-58a3-a0e4-daa55621d3c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320451} {:answer/id #uuid "68f85aa4-9ff4-5d41-b9a6-047f340b4436" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320452} {:answer/id #uuid "d781e2b1-7b73-53f5-aa5e-9598d05ee3c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320453}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22c091c6-f7b5-5527-b8b1-c873c7d06dd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320450} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe6da3f2-ab9e-5fc4-b2f0-3c180a33303a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320448}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "067c622d-d840-5844-b7eb-4a94820f230e" :question/math-academy-id "q-114272" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=({x}^{2}-4x+6){e}^{x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty )$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,\\infty )$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(-\\infty ,0)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{2}-4x+6){e}^{x}+(2x-4){e}^{x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-2x+2){e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =({x}^{2}-2x+2){e}^{x}+(2x-2){e}^{x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{e}^{x} & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{x}>0$ and ${x}^{2}>0$ for all real values $x\\ne 0,$ the inequality ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ has the solution $(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ).$ So $f(x)$ is concave up on the interval $(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality ${x}^{2}{e}^{x}<0,$ which does not have solutions. So $f(x)$ is concave down nowhere.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293701 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "437c168a-6799-5d13-bd9b-031290a84da6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals of Concavity of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae1c2967-fe66-5164-8106-1c48cf85e021" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293703} {:answer/id #uuid "98153d91-ae04-593a-acd3-4afb60a74acf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293704} {:answer/id #uuid "5bbd670c-e59b-51e5-8b48-a6c89b0f0fed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293705} {:answer/id #uuid "a0ab2773-bf4c-52c2-ba6c-89081c9bdd5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293706} {:answer/id #uuid "a91c2336-be0d-54a9-85f2-4ccd2edc27d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293707}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a91c2336-be0d-54a9-85f2-4ccd2edc27d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293707} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f1e316f-78a1-538e-a241-00deb8b4f4bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293702}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b0fa5633-3d2b-5f5b-a938-ef10da4c0590" :question/math-academy-id "q-114277" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+6\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ defined on $x\\in (0,\\infty ).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(0,1)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,1)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(1,\\infty )$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}+\\frac{6}{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =6x-\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6({x}^{3}-1)}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6({x}^{3}-1)}{{x}^{2}} & >0 \\\\ {x}^{3}-1 & >0 \\\\ {x}^{3} & >1 \\\\ x & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that ${x}^{2}>0.$\n\nHence, ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ when $x>1.$ So the graph is concave up on the interval $(1,\\infty ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality ${x}^{3}-1<0,$ which has the solution $(0,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \" II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184546 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b47fa40d-613a-5e9b-ba07-c9057dd1a96e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals on Which a Function Is Concave Up or Down"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3eb5451-e205-5b35-9824-99c2456d20e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function is concave up nowhere." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181772} {:answer/id #uuid "0c9edf5c-e2b2-520b-873a-4ffc4b24637b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181773} {:answer/id #uuid "2381c639-8b21-5a6e-acfb-e7c3f88bfcbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181774} {:answer/id #uuid "563bd63c-f259-5b3a-873b-3fba61a4d3e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1, 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181775} {:answer/id #uuid "605d6def-7586-52c2-aec4-d59ad36ff1ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181776}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c9edf5c-e2b2-520b-873a-4ffc4b24637b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181773} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc982674-a9e2-58d9-96a3-23989b918771" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181771}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "649986b4-08b2-5319-8f70-a6b70ebe2bc8" :question/math-academy-id "q-22794" :question/problem "Determine the largest interval on which the function $f(x)=2x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)$ is concave up." :question/worked-solution "First, we notice that the domain of the function is $x\\in (-1,\\infty ).$\n\nA function $f(x)$ is concave up on an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nFirst, let's calculate the second derivative of $f(x)=2x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =2-\\frac{1}{1+x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find where the function is concave up, we solve the inequality ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}>0\n$$\n\nNotice that for $x\\ne -1$ the second derivative is always positive since both the numerator and denominator are positive. Therefore, we conclude that $f(x)$ is concave up on its entire domain, $(-1,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320454 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b47fa40d-613a-5e9b-ba07-c9057dd1a96e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals on Which a Function Is Concave Up or Down"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77f12766-02a7-5d01-9b00-5b711d6e4db3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-\\frac{2}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320456} {:answer/id #uuid "50506176-f62c-555e-a496-23c1d6f3b74e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,\\frac{2}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320457} {:answer/id #uuid "56df065e-7270-5318-8aee-81f39434623a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320458} {:answer/id #uuid "2677ea65-0bb7-53aa-8ebb-9143c6ec3896" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{3},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320459} {:answer/id #uuid "1a451d10-e2af-532f-a4d7-b19989a34adc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,\\frac{1}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320460}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2677ea65-0bb7-53aa-8ebb-9143c6ec3896" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{3},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320459} :answer-field/id #uuid "900a2d18-6343-53b5-bfe4-98b15da2ad5d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320455}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "61f4c5d5-e624-5f18-a1ff-0dcd8d62b8ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-2458" :question/problem "Determine the largest interval on which the function $f(x)=-{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-11$ is concave down." :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x)$ is concave down on an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nFirst, let's calculate the second derivative of $f(x)=-{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-11:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-3{x}^{2}+4x \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-6x+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find where the function is concave down, we solve the inequality ${f}^{″}(x)<0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6x+4 & <0 \\\\ 6x & >4 \\\\ x & >\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is concave down on $(\\frac{2}{3},\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320461 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b47fa40d-613a-5e9b-ba07-c9057dd1a96e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals on Which a Function Is Concave Up or Down"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd5add92-3bcc-5ace-be07-bb89f6fd06ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\infty ,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320463} {:answer/id #uuid "c76c6d9d-183f-589f-8653-fd4381ebcde5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function is concave up nowhere." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320464} {:answer/id #uuid "cb0c95dc-8676-5961-96b8-e5b9a286c8f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320465} {:answer/id #uuid "e77c9aae-45aa-549a-bde0-9138bd1adeae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320466} {:answer/id #uuid "182fb936-49db-555b-bf5d-51c11841e965" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320467}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "182fb936-49db-555b-bf5d-51c11841e965" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320467} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4e02abe-fafd-5991-a640-68b2b89b87f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320462}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a1508161-8034-5831-a129-903a8a650526" :question/math-academy-id "q-2719" :question/problem "Determine the largest interval on which the function $f(x)=3{x}^{2}-9x+6$ is concave up" :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x)$ is concave up on an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nFirst, let's calculate the second derivative of $f(x)=3{x}^{2}-9x+6:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =6x-9 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find where the function is concave up, we solve the inequality ${f}^{″}(x)>0.$ Notice that ${f}^{″}(x)=6>0$ for all values of $x.$ \n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184685 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1b60735-9dae-5c23-991d-0241c208b1b7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals of Concavity Given the Graph of the Second Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "863515ff-8ffa-5b28-9838-02b3f2ee569d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-4, 0)$ and $(0, 4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183005} {:answer/id #uuid "8974190b-3ce8-54d8-b280-96db74be70b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0, 4)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183006} {:answer/id #uuid "e526471f-734d-50c7-8111-39708dfcf1c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(- ∞,-4)$ and $(0, 4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183007} {:answer/id #uuid "f4b7fc8b-8aa3-5225-8b1a-b1142bd31a58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-4, 0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183008} {:answer/id #uuid "3f44e70e-9e92-5956-86ee-8ffee243e87a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-4, 0)$ and $(4, ∞)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183009}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8974190b-3ce8-54d8-b280-96db74be70b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0, 4)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183006} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c59ab91-fe4f-538c-9b7e-7e96d0c2a5ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183004}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f627add3-7eef-5ed1-b10b-a3822778f5e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-31214" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/assets/9fe57341b631d34a9dfa41619a29f816b00b41b3b07f450fd333808230835e2c.png)\n\nThe graph of the second derivative $y={f}^{″}(x)$ of the function $f(x)$ is shown above. \nFind the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave up." :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x)$ is concave up over an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nOn the given graph of $y={f}^{″}(x),$ we can see that ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ on the interval $(0,4)$ only. \n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is concave up on the interval $(0,4)$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320468 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1b60735-9dae-5c23-991d-0241c208b1b7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals of Concavity Given the Graph of the Second Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad264db1-5529-538f-8016-ec81fa14e725" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320470} {:answer/id #uuid "e9b37a24-5046-5f7e-a66e-088190e134db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320471} {:answer/id #uuid "ca3deccf-a9d7-5fad-9be3-8b1b84f41106" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320472} {:answer/id #uuid "46016a84-8d64-5e42-b17f-5926e610ce6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320473} {:answer/id #uuid "c281e42a-ffbd-5a2f-8740-11952dc63d89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320474}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca3deccf-a9d7-5fad-9be3-8b1b84f41106" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320472} :answer-field/id #uuid "bbba0dd4-3ab9-5461-9f7b-85df9f9b0720" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320469}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b3c86bc2-98d4-5fb5-acf1-a49b3c614ac3" :question/math-academy-id "q-31221" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/assets/5287302ea411868ac0d2f8d06e5487dabcb421f6e462e0af0db30e34c328b8d4.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y={g}^{″}(x),$ where the function $g(x)$ is defined on $[-5,5].$ Find the intervals on which the function $g(x)$ is concave up." :question/worked-solution "A function $g(x)$ is concave up over an interval $(a,b)$ if ${g}^{″}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nOn the given graph of $y={g}^{″}(x)$, we can see that ${g}^{″}(x)>0$ on the interval $(0,5)$ only. Therefore, the function $g(x)$ is concave up on the interval $(0,5)$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320475 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b47fa40d-613a-5e9b-ba07-c9057dd1a96e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals on Which a Function Is Concave Up or Down"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d7edf00-96b3-5ec0-af86-cd6468e0759e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320477}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d7edf00-96b3-5ec0-af86-cd6468e0759e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320477} :answer-field/id #uuid "628f8670-995e-536c-a534-9a03e1d5940a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320476} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba1f896c-e81a-5be4-b7d3-49657a24dec4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320479}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba1f896c-e81a-5be4-b7d3-49657a24dec4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320479} :answer-field/id #uuid "31cf0912-1e14-556e-a8cc-3fe0e293dd2d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320478}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4d764786-e782-55ee-95fb-818969f26457" :question/math-academy-id "q-344331" :question/problem "Determine the **largest** interval on which the function $f(x)=7x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)$ is concave up.\n\nThe largest interval where $f(x)$ is concave up is\n$(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we notice that the domain of the function is $x\\in (2,\\infty ).$\n\nA function $f(x)$ is concave up on an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nFirst, let's calculate the second derivative of $f(x)=7x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =7-\\frac{1}{x-2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find where the function is concave up, we solve the inequality ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}}>0\n$$\n\nNotice that for $x\\ne 2$ the second derivative is always positive since both the numerator and denominator are positive. Therefore, we conclude that the largest interval where $f(x)$ is concave up is $(\\,2,\\infty \\,).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320480 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b47fa40d-613a-5e9b-ba07-c9057dd1a96e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals on Which a Function Is Concave Up or Down"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "480293e6-fc6c-54b6-896a-579231a80508" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320482}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "480293e6-fc6c-54b6-896a-579231a80508" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320482} :answer-field/id #uuid "f82ef3b2-4fe9-56d1-92d3-e70dcff0fbbb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320481} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b3954b9-d708-5d91-8adc-dfa35c920151" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320484}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b3954b9-d708-5d91-8adc-dfa35c920151" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320484} :answer-field/id #uuid "28224180-a635-5de2-9040-4d515801a86c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320483}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "936f4aaf-184a-570f-bb32-26f39de342aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-344352" :question/problem "Determine the **largest** interval on which the function $f(x)=\\frac{3x+1}{x+4}$ is concave up.\n\nThe largest interval where $f(x)$ is concave up is\n$(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we notice that the domain of the function is $x\\ne -4.$\n\nA function $f(x)$ is concave up on an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nFirst, let's calculate the second derivative of $f(x)=\\frac{3x+1}{x+4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{3(x+4)-(3x+1)}{(x+4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{(x+4)^{2}} \\\\  & =11(x+4)^{-2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-22(x+4)^{-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find where the function is concave up, we solve the inequality ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-22(x+4)^{-3} & >0 \\\\ (x+4)^{-3} & <0 \\\\ x+4 & <0 \\\\ x & <-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is concave up on $(\\,-\\infty ,-4\\,).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320485 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1b60735-9dae-5c23-991d-0241c208b1b7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals of Concavity Given the Graph of the Second Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c4e2c60-9165-5de2-a999-849a8f927e13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320487}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c4e2c60-9165-5de2-a999-849a8f927e13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320487} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9e8ce41-45ed-5eeb-b844-37dbc565c0cc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320486} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f1ffeb5-af63-59db-b1c7-767c916cdeff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320489}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f1ffeb5-af63-59db-b1c7-767c916cdeff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320489} :answer-field/id #uuid "997050a4-b25f-5b38-b8db-23b3a0ce17cb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320488}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2582ee8e-344a-5728-9b4e-ca914cf6725d" :question/math-academy-id "q-344777" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/assets/f03d1f23687273ff94bb2ea4ee45991600c75a237bf4c0a1fdb955d0f00b9b3d.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\nThe graph of the second derivative $y={f}^{″}(x)$ of the function $f(x)$ intersects the $x$-axis at the points $x=-2$ and $x=3,$ as shown above. Find the interval on which the function $f(x)$ is concave down.\n\n$f(x)$  is concave down on the interval\n$(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x)$ is concave down over an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nFrom the plotted graph of $y={f}^{″}(x)$, we can  see that ${f}^{″}(x)<0$ on the interval   $(-2,3)$ \n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is concave down on the interval $(\\,-2,3\\,).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320490 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "437c168a-6799-5d13-bd9b-031290a84da6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals of Concavity of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "071199a4-c541-520b-a754-ef475c1124d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320492} {:answer/id #uuid "2ebbe0c5-c58e-57ee-bd17-a9a6110131d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320493} {:answer/id #uuid "1fe041b9-ed27-5f4b-b0f5-d41c8c4dcafc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320494} {:answer/id #uuid "287708a4-6902-5218-b5cf-4db66cd59ab5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320495} {:answer/id #uuid "a9fe613d-89a4-55e6-aab9-5fc2bcd7e307" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320496}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9fe613d-89a4-55e6-aab9-5fc2bcd7e307" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320496} :answer-field/id #uuid "817c1966-13a6-50c5-ade6-a5fa4e9b965a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320491}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e13c31ca-a0e6-5bea-ba02-5659bc22c857" :question/math-academy-id "q-79039" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+5x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(2,\\infty )$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(2,\\infty )$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,2)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}-12x+5 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =6x-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x-12 & >0\\,⟹\\,x>2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the function is concave up on $(2,\\infty ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $6x-12<0,$ which has the solution $(-\\infty ,2).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320497 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "437c168a-6799-5d13-bd9b-031290a84da6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals of Concavity of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60db634f-e851-55af-850b-10a962db3dc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320499} {:answer/id #uuid "90b76227-84ab-5798-b9a8-ba9165a87e2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320500} {:answer/id #uuid "d2290509-3e66-532c-950d-d486f6008f56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320501} {:answer/id #uuid "4ac9af74-1f95-5e9c-a233-785b35cbb66c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320502} {:answer/id #uuid "a63c466e-9e14-57bd-a799-0366ff57b8c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320503}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ac9af74-1f95-5e9c-a233-785b35cbb66c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320502} :answer-field/id #uuid "7170cab7-15c3-5191-9bc8-40652e2e7f6f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320498}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5f04afe1-6ba3-5a8c-bdc7-0d8962a4537a" :question/math-academy-id "q-79040" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}-2x$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,2)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,0)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(0,\\infty )$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =4{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}-2 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =12{x}^{2}-24x=12x(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x(x-2) & >0 \\\\ x(x-2) & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality by considering the graph of $y=x(x-2).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/assets/cb06747b13b36bc1b53f96df8d1345332200ecb8b7728342d7086963b51d86f5.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $x(x-2)>0$ when $x<0$ and $x>2.$ So the graph is concave up on the intervals  $(-\\infty ,0)$ and $(2,\\infty ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $x(x-2)<0,$ which has the solution $(0,2).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}]}
